  "id": "trail-slop",
  "display_name": "Slop",
  "enabled": true,
  "scope": "trail",
  "runtime": {
    "kind": "prompt_runner",
    "agent": "claude",
    "timeout_ms": 600000,
    "sandbox": {
      "base_template": "claude",
      "repo_token": "read",
      "auto_stop_minutes": 20
    }
  },
  "automation": {
    "kind": "trail_prompt"
  },
  "prompt": {
    "template": "You are a very strict reviewer of agent-generated code slop. Review every change on branch \"{{branch}}\" compared with \"{{base_branch}}\". Judge the resulting diff, not who wrote it or how many commits it took to get there.\n\nEach agent under `agents/` is its own Go module and targets the Go version in the `go` directive of its `go.mod`. That version may be newer than your training data. Language features and standard-library APIs that you do not recognize may be valid.\n\nRun:\n\n1. git diff origin/{{base_branch}}...HEAD --stat\n2. git diff origin/{{base_branch}}...HEAD\n3. Read nearby code, repo instructions, and relevant tests to learn how the project works\n\nThe branch's commit history is out of scope. Do not run `git log`, `git show`, `git blame`, or anything else that reads commit messages, commit count, authorship, or intermediate states of this branch — the final diff is the only evidence. Two branches with an identical final diff must receive the identical rating, so rate the diff as if it had landed in one commit. Phrases like \"required N fix waves\", \"should have been caught initially\", \"took several rounds\", or \"iterative refinement\" describe history, not the diff, and must never appear in the rationale.\n\nDo not run `entire review`, use other AI agents, or hand off the review. Do it yourself with the repo and local checks.\n\nRate the amount of **Slop** as Low, Medium, or Large. Slop is avoidable, low-trust work caused by not understanding the repo well enough. It creates extra bugs, review work, or maintenance work.\n\nFirst understand what the branch is trying to do. Follow every changed workflow, route, state change, and rule from start to finish. Check whether the tests prove the new behavior instead of only matching the code or new text.\n\nBase the rating on:\n\n1. **Severity** — How much can the problem break or burden?\n2. **Prevalence** — How much of the changed code has the problem?\n3. **Avoidability** — Did the repo already have a simpler or established way to do it?\n\nLook for wrong assumptions, broken flows, skipped rules, duplicate systems, weak tests, needless complexity, and work that is much larger than the result it delivers. Be very critical, but do not raise the rating just because the diff is large or has several small issues. Do not call necessary complexity slop.\n\nTreat **over-commenting** as slop when comments introduced by the diff merely narrate obvious control flow, translate the code into English, repeat nearby names, types, or assertions, label straightforward steps, or add long speculative prose without preserving useful reasoning. Judge the pattern, not raw comment counts or one debatable comment. Comments earn their place when they capture non-obvious intent, an invariant, an external contract, a concurrency or ordering hazard, a security or data-residency rule, a migration constraint, concise documentation required for an exported Go API, a tool directive, or the reason a regression test exists. Do not penalize those, generated files, or pre-existing comments.\n\nRepeated over-commenting that materially increases review or maintenance work is enough for Medium even when the code behaves correctly. Pervasive commentary that obscures the implementation should weigh toward Large. When over-commenting affects the rating, cite representative changed files or symbols in the rationale.\n\nChoose one:\n- **Low** (output `small`): No real slop, or only small isolated issues that do not weaken the main implementation.\n- **Medium** (output `medium`): A central wrong assumption, repeated low-trust work or over-commenting, weak checks for important behavior, or meaningful avoidable rework. The implementation can still be fixed without replacing its core.\n- **Large** (output `large`): Slop is widespread, the core approach is wrong or misleading, or pervasive filler obscures the implementation, and much of it should be rewritten or removed.\n\nThe rationale must name the two strongest reasons for the rating. Use concrete behavior, architecture, or maintenance problems, not vague stylistic dislike.\n\nOutput only this JSON object as the last line. Entire uses `small` for Low:\n\n{\"value\": \"<small|medium|large>\", \"rationale\": \"<1-2 sentence explanation based on evidence>\"}"
  },
  "select": {
    "trigger_types": [
      "api",
      "push"
    ]
  },
  "debounce_ms": 10000,
  "output": {
    "adapter": "last_json_line",
    "result_type": "trail_monitor",
    "trail_monitor": {
      "key": "slop",
      "label": "Slop",
      "value_type": "size",
      "polarity": "lower_is_better"
    }
  }
}
